.TH VAULT-UNLOCK 1 "October 2026" "vault" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vault-unlock, vault-lock \- remember the unlocked key for a while, or forget it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vault unlock
.RB [ \-\-for
.IR DURATION ]
.RB [ \-\-idle ]
.RB [ \-\-key
.IR PATH ]
.br
.B vault lock
.RB [ \-\-key
.IR PATH ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B unlock
asks for the passphrase, checks it, and starts a helper process, much like
.BR ssh\-agent (1),
that holds the unlocked key in memory until \fIDURATION\fR has passed
(default 5m). Until then every command using the same key file skips the
passphrase prompt. By default the deadline is fixed when you unlock and
using the key does not extend it; with \fB\-\-idle\fR it is \fIDURATION\fR
after the last command that used the key. Running
.B unlock
again replaces the helper with a fresh one.
.PP
.B lock
tells the helper to wipe the key and exit at once.
.PP
The key reaches the helper over a pipe and is never written to disk. Its
memory is locked against swapping and excluded from core dumps; on Linux the
helper is also not debuggable. It answers only on a Unix socket inside
\fI$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/vault-UID\fR (or the temporary directory), a directory
that must belong to this user with mode 0700. Rebooting ends it too.
If the helper cannot lock its memory, disable core dumps or (on Linux) make
itself undebuggable, it refuses the key and
.B unlock
fails, saying why. The helper is the same program, started with the
passphrase variables removed from its environment. Helpers started by
earlier (Go) versions of vault are compatible and can be used and locked.
.PP
Not available on Windows, where every command asks for the passphrase.
.PP
While the key is unlocked, any program running as this user can use it, and
it is whichever key the passphrase opened, real or decoy. Lock before
handing the machine to anyone.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-for " DURATION"
How long to remember the key, such as 90s, 5m or 2h. Default 5m.
.TP
.B \-\-idle
Count \fIDURATION\fR from the last time a command used the key, rather than
from the unlock, so the key stays while you keep working.
.PP
See also \fB\-\-key\fR in
.BR vault (1).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VAULT_PASSPHRASE
Passphrase for
.BR unlock .
When set for any other command, it is used instead of the remembered key.
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Where the helper's socket directory goes.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vault (1),
.BR ssh\-agent (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Andy Dixon <vault@dixon.cx>, https://www.dixon.cx
